Tableau Performance Optimization Techniques

1. Use Extracts Instead of Live Connections
Use Tableau Extracts (.hyper) when real-time data is not required. Extracts often improve performance by reducing repeated queries against the source database.
Example: Superstore sales analysis using an extract.

2. Reduce the Number of Records
Filter unnecessary dates, regions, categories, and historical records at the data source level when appropriate.
Example: Analyze the latest three years instead of 15 years.

3. Reduce the Number of Worksheets
Every worksheet on a dashboard can add rendering and query overhead. Remove unnecessary charts and avoid duplicating similar worksheets.

4. Optimize Calculated Fields
Simplify nested IF statements, avoid redundant calculations, and move suitable calculations to the database or data-preparation layer.
Example: Precompute profit margin when appropriate.

5. Optimize Filters
Use only necessary filters. High-cardinality filters, such as Customer Name or Order ID, can be expensive, particularly when showing all values.
6. Optimize Joins and Relationships
Remove unnecessary tables, avoid accidental many-to-many row multiplication, and validate join keys and data grain.
7. Simplify Dashboard Design
Avoid excessive marks, complex visualizations, too many containers, and unnecessary dashboard objects.
8. Use Performance Recording
Identify whether delays come from queries, calculations, layout, or rendering before making changes.

9. Use Context Filters only when they help the query plan; they are not automatically faster.
10. Optimize LOD expressions such as {FIXED}, {INCLUDE}, and {EXCLUDE}.
11. Reduce the number of quick filters that display all values.https://codeshare.io/
12. Use efficient SQL queries and database indexes where appropriate.
13. Reduce unnecessary dashboard actions and cross-filtering.
14. Limit the number of marks displayed in maps and detailed tables.
15. Optimize Tableau Server or Tableau Cloud extracts, concurrency, caching, and data-source performance.
